WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:05.700 --> 00:00:10.032
Hello. I am going to talk to you about the semi-conservative

00:00:10.033 --> 00:00:16.632
replication of DNA. Not so much about the technical details but about how Frank Stahl and I

00:00:16.633 --> 00:00:20.666
ended up doing the experiment that showed that DNA

00:00:20.667 --> 00:00:23.332
replicates by the two chains coming apart,

00:00:23.333 --> 00:00:27.332
each making a new copy and then you get two,

00:00:27.333 --> 00:00:30.799
each of which has one old chain and one new one.

00:00:30.800 --> 00:00:34.199
It is hard to know where to start any particular story

00:00:34.200 --> 00:00:37.999
but let me start with the publication in 1953

00:00:38.000 --> 00:00:41.966
of the paper by Watson and Crick. There were actually two papers

00:00:41.967 --> 00:00:45.032
in Nature. The first was about the structure

00:00:45.033 --> 00:00:50.499
which was based on model building and a little bit of information from X-ray diffraction

00:00:50.500 --> 00:00:54.299
The X-ray diffraction certainly did not tell you the structure.

00:00:54.300 --> 00:00:58.466
It told you something about the repeat distance along the helix.

00:00:58.467 --> 00:01:02.166
It told you that it was a helix and very little more.

00:01:02.167 --> 00:01:04.899
The model building was model building,

00:01:04.900 --> 00:01:07.432
so the structure certainly wasn't proven.

00:01:07.433 --> 00:01:10.666
This was a proposal and many people didn't believe it

00:01:10.667 --> 00:01:13.332
or maybe didn't even pay any attention to it.

00:01:13.333 --> 00:01:22.866
The second paper proposed how the molecule might replicate.  The two chains would separate, each would guide on its surface

00:01:22.867 --> 00:01:25.366
the formation of a new chain, so you would

00:01:25.367 --> 00:01:28.099
end up with two double helices, each one has

00:01:28.100 --> 00:01:30.766
one of the old chains and one brand new chain.

00:01:30.767 --> 00:01:33.666
That's called semi-conservative replication.

00:01:33.667 --> 00:01:40.032
At Caltech, Max Delbruck who knew Jim Watson and was in correspondence with him

00:01:40.033 --> 00:01:44.166
was pessimistic about this replication scheme.

00:01:44.167 --> 00:01:47.232
Pessimistic about semi-conservative replication.

00:01:47.233 --> 00:01:50.632
The chains were wound around each other, so to get them apart

00:01:50.633 --> 00:01:55.599
unless you break them, you would have to unwind the basic double helix

00:01:55.600 --> 00:01:56.899
to get the two arms separate.

00:01:56.900 --> 00:02:01.366
Max thought this would be impossible, hydronamically impossible.

00:02:01.367 --> 00:02:05.699
And so he proposed that maybe the two chains separated by a system of

00:02:05.700 --> 00:02:09.399
breaking every several nucleotides along

00:02:09.400 --> 00:02:12.566
the chain. And he and Gunther Stent published a paper

00:02:12.567 --> 00:02:15.899
which proposed three different methods for DNA replication.

00:02:15.900 --> 00:02:19.966
Semi-conservative, as  Watson and Crick had predicted,

00:02:19.967 --> 00:02:22.932
conservative, in which at least conceptually maybe there was

00:02:22.933 --> 00:02:26.299
a way that a double helix could guide the formation of

00:02:26.300 --> 00:02:28.632
another double helix just like it nearby

00:02:28.633 --> 00:02:33.032
and so you would never have to separate the chains at all.

00:02:33.033 --> 00:02:37.066
You'd have a brand new double helix and a completely old double helix, and that would

00:02:37.067 --> 00:02:39.399
be the act of replication.

00:02:39.400 --> 00:02:47.599
And then dispersive, the third way, break the single chains, separate the pieces, and then put everything back together again.

00:02:47.600 --> 00:02:52.799
I visited Max in his office. I was in Chemistry.

00:02:52.800 --> 00:02:55.032
I was a student of Linus Pauling.

00:02:55.033 --> 00:02:57.132
Max was over in Biology, and he told me

00:02:57.133 --> 00:03:01.566
about this problem, and it occurred to me that maybe one could

00:03:01.567 --> 00:03:06.132
do an experiment to find out the mode of replication of DNA

00:03:06.133 --> 00:03:08.499
based on the use of heavy isotopes.

00:03:08.500 --> 00:03:12.266
I wish I had time to tell you about why I thought about heavy isotopes

00:03:12.267 --> 00:03:16.366
but it did have to do with taking a course which was a piece of luck

00:03:16.367 --> 00:03:19.332
Linus Pauling's course on the nature of the chemical bond.

00:03:19.333 --> 00:03:23.566
in which deuterium and hydrogen bonds played an important role.

00:03:23.567 --> 00:03:28.999
Nevertheless, the idea was to label DNA with something heavy,

00:03:29.000 --> 00:03:33.166
I don't think I thought about, oh yes, it was deuterium I thought about at the time.

00:03:33.167 --> 00:03:37.366
Then to label it by growing bacteria or phages

00:03:37.367 --> 00:03:39.999
in heavy medium, deuterium medium,

00:03:40.000 --> 00:03:42.499
and then switch the growth to light medium

00:03:42.500 --> 00:03:45.666
and then put all this in the centrifuge and look so see where the

00:03:45.667 --> 00:03:49.399
DNA went-up to the top if you adjusted the density right

00:03:49.400 --> 00:03:54.099
if it was all light, down to the bottom if it was all heavy,

00:03:54.100 --> 00:03:57.166
and in the middle if it was half heavy and half light.

00:03:57.167 --> 00:04:01.299
That's an oversimplification, but that's the way I thought about the experiment

00:04:01.300 --> 00:04:05.466
at that  time. This was around 1954 sometime.

00:04:05.467 --> 00:04:08.832
I then went to Woods Hole as a teaching assistant

00:04:08.833 --> 00:04:14.066
for Jim Watson (he had lived at Caltech the previous term)

00:04:14.067 --> 00:04:17.432
in the physiology course.

00:04:17.433 --> 00:04:20.566
And one day as a teaching assistant in that course

00:04:20.567 --> 00:04:24.366
Jim Watson and Sydney Brenner, who were teaching the course,

00:04:24.367 --> 00:04:30.799
were in an upstairs room in a building called the Lillie building, the Marine Biological Lab,

00:04:30.800 --> 00:04:33.599
and Jim looked through the window and pointed down at a tree

00:04:33.600 --> 00:04:38.166
under which was sitting a man who was, you couldn't tell from a distance,

00:04:38.167 --> 00:04:40.599
but he was selling gin and tonic.

00:04:40.600 --> 00:04:45.732
He had a big jug of gin and a big thing of tonic, and some ice and some glasses and some limes,

00:04:45.733 --> 00:04:51.199
and he would sell gin and tonic to passersby, and with the profits he could buy some gin and tonic for himself.

00:04:51.200 --> 00:04:53.966
It was called the gin and tonic tree.

00:04:53.967 --> 00:04:57.499
But Jim was saying that this guy thought pretty much of himself,

00:04:57.500 --> 00:05:02.466
so let's give him a really tough experiment to do in the Physiology class,

00:05:02.467 --> 00:05:06.066
the famous Hershey-Chase experiment done by two people over a period of time,

00:05:06.067 --> 00:05:08.932
Hershey and Chase, and see if Stahl could do it all in one afternoon

00:05:08.933 --> 00:05:13.432
by himself. Well I thought that was pretty rotten to gang up on this poor guy

00:05:13.433 --> 00:05:18.232
So I went down and introduced myself to him and told him what lay in store for him.

00:05:18.233 --> 00:05:21.232
And he told me what he was doing in phage genetics

00:05:21.233 --> 00:05:23.932
and that he would be at Caltech the next year.

00:05:23.933 --> 00:05:29.099
So we agreed to try to do this experiment together, ourselves, on how DNA replicates

00:05:29.100 --> 00:05:33.832
when he got to Caltech.  I had to finish my X-ray crystallography first

00:05:33.833 --> 00:05:40.466
before Frank would let us start because he said it would be bad for my character to go ahead and start some new project

00:05:40.467 --> 00:05:43.099
when I hadn't finished my thesis work,

00:05:43.100 --> 00:05:44.632
which was X-ray crystallography.

00:05:44.633 --> 00:05:47.699
Finally I got the X-ray crystallography done

00:05:47.700 --> 00:05:50.166
and we could start the experiment.

00:05:50.167 --> 00:05:52.866
Frank and I decided that we  should develop the method

00:05:52.867 --> 00:05:55.799
for doing this density gradient centrifugation

00:05:55.800 --> 00:05:59.999
and so we spent quite a long time, more than a year,

00:06:00.000 --> 00:06:04.299
developing a method that could separate macromolecules in a density gradient.

00:06:04.300 --> 00:06:07.099
And to do that we put in pure DNA

00:06:07.100 --> 00:06:10.532
into a centrifuge and turned it on to see what was happening

00:06:10.533 --> 00:06:14.666
and to our amazement a density gradient was forming

00:06:14.667 --> 00:06:16.466
before our very eyes.

00:06:16.467 --> 00:06:22.332
This was because the cesium ion is quite heavy, quite dense, and it

00:06:22.333 --> 00:06:26.299
tends to settle to the bottom in a powerful centrifugal field

00:06:26.300 --> 00:06:29.832
and the diffusion wants it to go back up to redistribute it

00:06:29.833 --> 00:06:34.832
and at equilibrium you have a density gradient in which there is more cesium chloride

00:06:34.833 --> 00:06:36.899
near the bottom than near the top.

00:06:36.900 --> 00:06:40.266
And so the density gradient forms automatically.

00:06:40.267 --> 00:06:41.432
We didn't expect this.

00:06:41.433 --> 00:06:44.999
We saw it happen, we thought we would have to make a preformed density gradient

00:06:45.000 --> 00:06:48.999
in the centrifuge cell

00:06:49.000 --> 00:06:51.466
but the centrifuge itself makes the density gradient.

00:06:51.467 --> 00:06:57.466
So to make a long story short, we grew bacteria in heavy nitrogen medium

00:06:57.467 --> 00:07:01.866
and then after many generation of growth, so that everything would

00:07:01.867 --> 00:07:07.832
be labeled with heavy nitrogen, we switched the bacteria, centrifuging them and resuspending them

00:07:07.833 --> 00:07:11.866
in a light medium and took samples at various times.

00:07:11.867 --> 00:07:18.466
The first experiment Frank had warned me I would mix it up if I did both directions at once,

00:07:18.467 --> 00:07:20.532
if I did heavy to light and light to heavy.

00:07:20.533 --> 00:07:25.832
And I said, "no, I'll color code the tubes," and I mixed it up completely.

00:07:25.833 --> 00:07:30.932
So it was unclear exactly which tubes were which.  The second experiment

00:07:30.933 --> 00:07:35.466
we labeled experiment number one and that is what we published,

00:07:35.467 --> 00:07:38.766
along with experiment number 2, which was a repeat.

00:07:38.767 --> 00:07:42.299
And what we found of course was that

00:07:42.300 --> 00:07:46.832
the bacteria at one generation had only one kind of DNA.

00:07:46.833 --> 00:07:49.866
It had a density half way between heavy and light.

00:07:49.867 --> 00:07:53.632
And at the second bacterial generation, there were two kinds of DNA,

00:07:53.633 --> 00:07:56.899
half heavy, and fully light.

00:07:56.900 --> 00:08:01.199
Now we had to write this paper up. Actually we were rather sluggish in writing it up

00:08:01.200 --> 00:08:04.866
and so  Max Delbruck took us to the marine lab

00:08:04.867 --> 00:08:07.999
at Corona del Mar, and locked us up in a tower room.

00:08:08.000 --> 00:08:11.832
He literally did. Mary Delbruck, Max's wife, would bring us meals,

00:08:11.833 --> 00:08:15.166
but then locked the door again,

00:08:15.167 --> 00:08:16.432
until we- and there was a typewriter-

00:08:16.433 --> 00:08:20.199
until we produced a draft manuscript, which we did.

00:08:20.200 --> 00:08:22.032
But there was a question how to write this up.

00:08:22.033 --> 00:08:24.666
There were two ways that we discussed.

00:08:24.667 --> 00:08:28.832
One way is to start with a hypothesis, the Watson-Crick hypothesis,

00:08:28.833 --> 00:08:31.866
and say here's a test, we do this experiment.

00:08:31.867 --> 00:08:36.432
and see if it works out the way they said it should.

00:08:36.433 --> 00:08:41.866
And that is certainly one way to do an experiment, and Richard Feynman, who was very close to students in those days

00:08:41.867 --> 00:08:47.266
He would come over to our parties and so on.  He thought that we should write it up that way.

00:08:47.267 --> 00:08:53.632
The other way would be to write up exactly what your experiment said, no more, no less

00:08:53.633 --> 00:08:55.999
without reference to any hypotheses at all,

00:08:56.000 --> 00:09:00.499
and then at the very end say whether it may agree with some hypothesis.

00:09:00.500 --> 00:09:07.032
We chose the last way, partly because we thought, well, if you are trying to test a hypothesis,

00:09:07.033 --> 00:09:10.099
the only way to really be sure that you are going to be right

00:09:10.100 --> 00:09:13.866
is to know all possible hypotheses.

00:09:13.867 --> 00:09:16.199
And since nobody can know all possible hypotheses

00:09:16.200 --> 00:09:18.966
just because your experiment might agree with one of them

00:09:18.967 --> 00:09:21.066
doesn't prove that that is the right hypothesis.

00:09:21.067 --> 00:09:24.666
There could be another one that your experiment agrees with

00:09:24.667 --> 00:09:27.699
So it seemed to us more elegant to write our paper

00:09:27.700 --> 00:09:30.632
up in terms of subunits, and only at the end say,

00:09:30.633 --> 00:09:35.432
"ah, these subunits could be the single chains of the Watson-Crick model,"

00:09:35.433 --> 00:09:37.732
which of course they were. So that is what we did.

00:09:37.733 --> 00:09:42.332
And then this diagram here shows the result in terms of subunits

00:09:42.333 --> 00:09:48.366
not DNA chains. What we did was blessed by a lot of accidents.

00:09:48.367 --> 00:09:50.066
The accident of being at Caltech,

00:09:50.067 --> 00:09:52.332
the accident of meeting each other at Woods Hole,

00:09:52.333 --> 00:09:55.899
the accident of having Max Delbruck impress you

00:09:55.900 --> 00:09:59.232
with his deep pessimism that DNA couldn't possibly replicate

00:09:59.233 --> 00:10:02.866
the Watson and Crick work the way it does.

00:10:02.867 --> 00:10:06.532
And finally the accident of finding out that the centrifuge itself

00:10:06.533 --> 00:10:09.199
will make a density gradient, you don't have to

00:10:09.200 --> 00:10:11.032
make a pre-existing one.

00:10:11.033 --> 00:10:14.432
The effect of this experiment is worth saying something about.

00:10:14.433 --> 00:10:16.466
When the DNA structure was proposed, a lot

00:10:16.467 --> 00:10:18.032
of people didn't believe it.

00:10:18.033 --> 00:10:20.599
And there were not a lot of references to it in the literature.

00:10:20.600 --> 00:10:24.132
for the first several years after 1953.

00:10:24.133 --> 00:10:27.766
After all, it was based on model building, which doesn't prove anything.

00:10:27.767 --> 00:10:31.132
It looked so beautiful that some people were convinced

00:10:31.133 --> 00:10:34.632
that it had to be right because it looked so right.

00:10:34.633 --> 00:10:40.132
Other people I think were convinced that it couldn't be right because it looked too good to be true.

00:10:40.133 --> 00:10:44.299
In any case, it was just a model.

00:10:44.300 --> 00:10:48.732
The evidence from the X-ray diffraction was really not very supportive.

00:10:48.733 --> 00:10:52.132
It showed that the repeat distance was a certain

00:10:52.133 --> 00:10:54.466
distance and that it was helical, but you couldn't

00:10:54.467 --> 00:10:58.066
deduce any of the details from those early X-ray pictures.

00:10:58.067 --> 00:11:02.732
I think the effect  of our experiment wasn't really a discovery,

00:11:02.733 --> 00:11:07.732
It was a psychological effect. It made the DNA seem real. Suddenly you could

00:11:07.733 --> 00:11:09.899
see bands in a centrifuge.

00:11:09.900 --> 00:11:14.066
that were behaving just in the way Watson and Crick said they should.

00:11:14.067 --> 00:11:18.132
And  I think that the main value of the experiment was

00:11:18.133 --> 00:11:21.599
that it had the psychological value of convincing a lot of people

00:11:21.600 --> 00:11:24.966
that the DNA  structure had to be right.

00:11:24.967 --> 00:11:27.666
Let me say something about that molecule.

00:11:27.667 --> 00:11:34.399
This molecule essentially gave orders to a whole period in the development of molecular biology.

00:11:34.400 --> 00:11:36.532
Here is this double helix, standing there,

00:11:36.533 --> 00:11:41.432
and it's saying, "Here I am. I have two chains. Go find out how I replicate."

00:11:41.433 --> 00:11:44.166
"Here I am. I have four kinds of bases."

00:11:44.167 --> 00:11:47.866
"Go figure out how that is converted into making proteins."

00:11:47.867 --> 00:11:52.699
"I sit in the nucleus. Proteins are made out in the cytoplasm in eukaryotes."

00:11:52.700 --> 00:11:56.699
"Go figure out what it is that takes this information from me out to the cytoplasm."

00:11:56.700 --> 00:12:01.299
"My information being made up of these bases changes once in a while."

00:12:01.300 --> 00:12:04.266
"That's called mutation. Go figure out how that happens."

00:12:04.267 --> 00:12:08.332
"Every once in a while there is something called genetic recombination."

00:12:08.333 --> 00:12:11.532
"Go figure out how I am..." What I am trying to say here

00:12:11.533 --> 00:12:14.599
is that if I showed you a model of say a polysaccharide,

00:12:14.600 --> 00:12:17.366
would it tell you what experiment you should do next?

00:12:17.367 --> 00:12:20.366
This molecule, DNA, is like the wizard of Oz

00:12:20.367 --> 00:12:24.132
standing there (except unlike the wizard, this is a true molecule)

00:12:24.133 --> 00:12:29.832
standing there and telling you essentially the whole agenda

00:12:29.833 --> 00:12:32.232
for the future of science for the next 20 years.

00:12:32.233 --> 00:12:37.099
Now that's a completely different mood and attitude, I think,

00:12:37.100 --> 00:12:39.499
from the science that went before it

00:12:39.500 --> 00:12:42.832
and the biological science that came after it.

00:12:42.833 --> 00:12:44.199
So that was currency of this time.

00:12:44.200 --> 00:13:08.800
It was the DNA molecule telling you what the problems were, and what you had to go out and solve.

